// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Red Shirt
// @category Death Tropes / Cast Filler
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Red_Shirt
// @domain   Narrative
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the nameless, set-filler crew member (named for the original Star Trek
// security officers) whose only job is to die — killed off to show the menace is deadly without losing
// anyone important. FRONTIER: the EXPENDABLE death — recognized by who dies, not how. The discriminator
// is the [+Expendable] tag on the slain party, bound on the target of a kill, which separates it from
// the death tropes that key on a SIGNIFICANT death (Death by Origin Story's loved one, Heroic Sacrifice's
// willing hero, Mercy Kill's loved one spared). A nobody dies so the threat is proven and a main lives.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"

assoc red_shirt -> [
  death_by_origin_story : "the expendable inverse of (a nobody, not a loved one)",
  the_worf_effect       : "kills to prove the menace where Worf only wounds to",
  heroic_sacrifice      : "an unchosen death unlike the willing"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// — founded here (the expendable filler character is the discriminator; promotion model, cf. the_wizard) —
prop Expendable   [+Narrative]   // set-filler — a nameless character who exists to be killed off
prop Lethal       [+Narrative]   // proven deadly — the menace the expendable death establishes

imply RedShirt -> [Object, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: an [+Expendable] character is the TARGET of a kill ([&Slays], agent=$threat), so phase-2
// re-checks [+Expendable] on the slain party (STYLE §8). Binding expendability on the victim is what
// parts this from every significant-death trope (which kill a loved one / a hero / a main): a Red Shirt
// is a nobody, and the death's job is to prove the threat — so the rule also tags the killer [+Lethal].
rule Red_Shirt {
  when:
    char $redshirt [+Expendable]
    evt  $death [&Slays(agent=$threat, target=$redshirt)]
  then:
    $death [+RedShirt]
    $threat [+Lethal]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Star Trek — the landing party (TV, 1966) ===
// The transporter shimmers and a landing party steps onto the alien surface: the Captain, the Doctor —
// and an ensign in a red security shirt nobody has met before. Within the minute the creature takes him:
// a flash, a scream, and he crumples while the named officers look on, unharmed. "He's dead, Jim." The
// nameless crewman existed for exactly this — to show the thing on this planet can kill, so the audience
// fears for the heroes who can't be. He is mourned for a beat and never mentioned again.

// ENSIGN RICKY — the nameless security crewman in the red shirt, met moments ago
char ensign_ricky [+Expendable]

// THE CREATURE — the planet's menace, deadliness yet unproven
char the_creature [+Monster]

// CAPTAIN KIRK — a main character, present and pointedly unharmed
char kirk [+Protagonist]

// EXT. ALIEN SURFACE - DAY (the landing site)
set alien_surface [+Exterior] [+Day]

scene the_landing_party {
  beat 1 {
    ensign_ricky @ alien_surface
    kirk @ alien_surface
  }
  beat 2 {
    evt the_kill [&Slays(agent=the_creature, target=ensign_ricky)]
    // Red_Shirt -> the_kill [+RedShirt]; the_creature [+Lethal] (the menace is proven, no main lost)
  }
}
